"WaveForms Application (https://files.digilent.com/manuals/WaveForms/3.25.1/main.html)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://files.digilent.com/manuals/WaveForms/3.25.1/main.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 501\nL0: # WaveForms Application\nL1: \nL2: ## 1. Getting Started with WaveForms\nL3: \nL4: Install the WaveForms software, if you haven't already. See 【0†Installer Details】 for more information.\nL5: \nL6: Connect your board to your PC using the USB connector and the supplied USB cable.\nL7: \nL8: Start the WaveForms application from the Start Menu > All Programs > Digilent > WaveForms Application > WaveForms.\nL9: \nL10: The application starts and connects to your board.\nL11: \nL12: 【1†Image】\nL13: ### 1.1. Troubleshoot WaveForms\nL14: \nL15: Only one application can be connected to one board at a time. If you get the message “The selected device is being used by another application”, check the taskbar for other applications in use.\nL16: \nL17: If the application is not working as expected, try starting the WaveForms application from the MS Windows Start Menu > All Programs > Digilent > WaveForms Application > WaveForms Safe Mode or the WaveForms application with “clear” parameter.\nL18: See the device specific troubleshooting for more information:\nL19: \nL20:   * 【2†Analog Discovery 3】\nL21:   * 【3†Analog Discovery Pro 2230】\nL22:   * 【4†Analog Discovery Pro 2440/2450】\nL23:   * 【5†Analog Discovery Pro 3250/3450】\nL24:   * 【6†Analog Discovery Pro 5250/5470/5490】\nL25:   * 【7†Digital Discovery】\nL26:   * 【8†Discovery Power Supplies 3340】\nL27:   * 【9†Eclypse Z7】\nL28:   * 【10†Analog Discovery】\nL29:   * 【11†Analog Discovery 2】\nL30:   * 【12†Electronics Explorer】\nL31:   * 【13†Sound Card】\nL32: ### 1.2. Select an Instrument\nL33: The WaveForms's main window Welcome tab (shown above) has buttons for each instrument: Scope (Oscilloscope), called Meter with DPS3340, Wavegen (Waveform Generator), (Power) Supplies, called DiscoveryPS when used with DPS3340 Select+Dual, Voltmeter, (Data) Logger, Logic (Analyzer), Patterns (Pattern Generator), Static I/O (Digital Input/Output), Spectrum (Analyzer), Network (Analyzer), Impedance (Analyzer), (Curve) Tracer, DMM (Digital Multimeter), Protocol and Script instruments.\nL34: The instruments can also be opened from the Welcome tab “+” (add) menu.\nL35: An instrument's button is disabled when the selected device or configuration does not support it.\nL36: \nL37: The Settings menu contains the 【14†Options】, the 【15†Device Manager】, and Manual Trigger.\nL38: \nL39: The Workspace's Open, Save, and Save as buttons allow the user to load or save WaveForms 【16†workspaces】.\nL40: When an instrument is closed, its state is saved and loaded when reopened. The New button creates a new workspace, which can be used to close the instruments that are currently open while also forgetting the last instrument configurations.\nL41: The status bar of the WaveForms main window displays the device name and serial number, as shown below. Clicking on this will open the Device Manager.\nL42: The following button opens the Device Options.\nL43: The status button opens a drop-down listing system monitor information, depending on device it shows voltages, temperature, power consumption...\nL44: The Run/Stop All buttons start or stop all the instruments. In case multiple tools are opened using the same resource, like Scope and Spectrum Analyzer, the last used one will be started. If Script tool is opened only this will be started.\nL45: The following two buttons switch between Tabbed and Docking Windows.\nL46: The last gear button opens the Application Options.\nL47: ### 1.3. The Workspace and Project\nL48: \nL49: The workspace refers to any open instruments and their current state. The workspace can be loaded and saved with the Open and Save/Save As buttons in the WaveForms's Welcome tab.\nL50: \nL51: The workspace and instrument project can be saved in one of the following modes selected by the save filter:\nL52:   * without acquisition: saves only the Oscilloscope reference channels and data in tabs, Logic Analyzer tabs, custom Wavegen waveforms, and Pattern Generator custom data.\nL53:   * with acquisition: also saves all buffered data in Oscilloscope and Logic Analyzer, captures in Spectrum, Network and Impedance Analyzer.\nL54: The workspace files are associated with WaveForms. When you open a workspace (double-click on a *.dwf3work file) and WaveForms is running, it will be opened with the last used application instance. Otherwise, if WaveForms is not currently running, it will open with a new application instance.\nL55: \nL56: The project refers to an instrument and its current state. The project can be loaded and saved with the Open and Save buttons in each instrument.\nL57: The project files are associated with WaveForms. When you open a project (double-click on a *.dwf3scope, *.dwf3wavegen, *dwf3logic, ... file) and WaveForms is running, it will be opened with the last used application instance. Otherwise, if WaveForms is not currently running, it will open with a new application instance.\nL58: \nL59: The New button closes the current project. It can also be used to clear the last settings of the instruments.\nL60: The Extract/Compress unpacks the workspace archive. It can be used to extract raw capture data and configuration files.\nL61: \nL62: The Compare can be used to compare multiple workspaces, listing capture device Serial Numbers, date-times, highlighting duplicate SNs.\nL63: ## 2. Options\nL64: \nL65: The Options window allows you to select various display and configuration preferences.\nL66: \nL67: To open the Options window, select the Settings / Options menu from the WaveForms window.\nL68: \nL69: 【17†Image】\nL70: \nL71: Application options:\nL72:   * Instrument windows: Specifies how the instrument windows are opened; it can be one of the following options:\nL73:     * Separate: separate windows.\nL74:     * Tabs: tabs in main window.\nL75:     * MDI: multiple document interface.\nL76:     * Docking: docked to main window.\nL77:   * Style: Selects the application GUI style. The available options depend on the system. It is recommended to restart the application after a change.\nL78:   * Analog/Digital color: Selects the color for analog or digital instrument plots.\nL79:   * Font: Selects the application font.\nL80:   * Script: Selects the font used in the code editors.\nL81:   * Locale: Selects the locale to be used for decimal symbol and list separator.\nL82:   * Digit Grouping: Selects the decimal digit grouping for Data, Event view time columns and few other place.\nL83:   * Lnguage: Selects the application language. But first, you need to place the translation file in the C:/Program Files/Digilent/WaveForms3/lang or /usr/share/digilent/waveforms/lang folder. By default, this folder contains an English reference file.\nL84:   * Dialog: Selects the file dialog to be operating system's native or custom style.\nL85:   * Update check: Selects update checking interval or disables it.\nL86: 【18†Image】\nL87: \nL88: Graphics options:\nL89:   * Plot width: Adjusts the default line thickness in plots. Custom width can be adjusted for each plot.\nL90:   * Transparency: Adjusts the plot transparency.\nL91:   * Graphics optimization: Adjusts the balance between quality and speed.\nL92:   * Curve: Selects the curve drawn between analog sample points, linear or smooth.\nL93:   * Animations: Selects the animation speed of value adjustments or disables the animation.\nL94:   * Mouse Drag: Enables or disable the mouse drag action on drawings scale and/or plot area.\nL95:   * Mouse Wheel: Enables or disable the mouse wheel action on drawings scale and/or plot area.\nL96:   * Mouse Up: Selects the mouse wheel direction.\nL97: 【19†Image】\nL98: \nL99: Device options:\nL100: See the device description for available options and details.\nL101:   * Trigger #: Trigger pin can be configured to be input (default) or to be driven by an instrument's trigger signal and also to be driven low or high for digital output purpose.\nL102:   * On Close: Selects the behavior on software disconnect from device:\nL103:     * Continue: Keep the instruments running, like Power Supplies, Wavegen and Patterns Generator outputs active after closing the application. Note that workspace/project saving and export dialog will stop the instruments.\nL104:     * Stop: Stop the instruments, but keep the device operational to prevent temperature drift on next connection.\nL105:     * Shutdown: Shutdown the device for minimal power consumption.\nL106:   * Shutdown: The device will be set to minimum power consumption after the specified time has passed since the application was disconnected, for 【20†ADP2440/ADP2450】.\nL107:   * Reconnect: Enable or disable the auto-reconnect to the device after connection drop or device reset.\nL108:   * LED brightness: Adjusts the LED brightness on Digital Discovery in percentage.\nL109:   * USB Limit: Specify the maximum USB current the device can consume, for Analog Discovery 1 and 2.\nL110:   * USB Power: Keep the USB supply ON even when connecting AUX supply, for Analog Discovery 2, 3. With this option the device won't stop when disconnecting the auxiliary supply.\nL111:   * Audio Output: Enable or disable the audio output, for Analog Discovery 1 and 2.\nL112:   * Wavegen Output: Enable or disable the Wavegen output relay, for 【21†ADP3450/ADP3250】, 【22†ADP2230】, 【20†ADP2440/ADP2450】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://files.digilent.com/datasheets/Analog-Discovery-3-Datasheet.pdf)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 26; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://files.digilent.com/datasheets/Analog-Discovery-3-Datasheet.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 734\nL0@P0: Analog Discovery 3 Datasheet\nL1@P0: 125 MS/s USB Oscilloscope, Waveform Generator, Logic Analyzer, and Variable Power Supply\nL2@P0: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL3@P0: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 1 of 26\nL4@P0: Highlighted Features\nL5@P0: • Two differential 14-bit oscilloscope channels with 9\nL6@P0: MHz bandwidth\nL7@P0: • Two 14-bit arbitrary waveform generator channels\nL8@P0: • 16 digital I/O channels for MSO and pattern\nL9@P0: generation functionality\nL10@P0: • Variable 0.5 to 5 V and -0.5 to -5 V power supplies\nL11@P0: • Modern USB-C connector for power and data\nL12@P0: connection to a host PC\nL13@P0: • Comprehensive software support through the\nL14@P0: WaveForms application and SDK\nL15@P0: • Ecosystem of adapters for a broad array of\nL16@P0: experiments\nL17@P0: Overview\nL18@P0: The Digilent Analog Discovery 3, featuring Texas Instruments™ components, is a multi-function test and\nL19@P0: measurement device that allows users to measure, visualize, generate, record, and control mixed signal circuits of\nL20@P0: all kinds. The low-cost Analog Discovery 3 is small enough to fit in your pocket, but powerful enough to replace a\nL21@P0: stack of lab equipment, providing engineering students, hobbyists, and electronics enthusiasts the freedom to work\nL22@P0: with analog and digital circuits in virtually any environment, in or out of the lab.\nL23@P0: Building off of Digilent’s Analog Discovery product line, including the wildly popular Analog Discovery 2, the AD3\nL24@P0: features a higher sample rate and bandwidth, USB C connectivity, and larger sample buffers, while maintaining\nL25@P0-1: backwards compatibility with existing projects and coursework.Analog Discovery 3 Datasheet – Rev 1, May 2025\nL26@P1: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL27@P1: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 2 of 26\nL28@P1: 1 Features\nL29@P1: Analog Inputs\nL30@P1: • Two differential input channels with 14-bit resolution and ±25 V input range\nL31@P1: • Up to 125 MS/s per channel, 9 MHz bandwidth increased to 30+ MHz with BNC adapter\nL32@P1: Analog Output\nL33@P1: • Two output channels with 14-bit resolution, ±5 V output range\nL34@P1: • Up to 125 MS/s per channel, 9 MHz bandwidth increased to 12 MHz with BNC adapter\nL35@P1: Digital I/O\nL36@P1: • 16 dynamically configurable digital input/output channels\nL37@P1: • 3.3 V CMOS\nL38@P1: • Up to 125 MS/s per channel\nL39@P1: Power Supplies\nL40@P1: • Two programmable power supplies, V+ and V-: 0.5 V to 5 V and -0.5 V to -5 V\nL41@P1: • Up to 800 mA per channel when used with an external power supply\nL42@P1: Additional Features\nL43@P1: • Configurable input filters and internal AWG to scope loopback paths\nL44@P1: • Additional software instruments including Spectrum, Network, Impedance, and Protocol Analyzers\nL45@P1: • Complex software views like FFTs, spectrograms, fully customizable math channels, lock-in amplifier\nL46@P1: channels, eye diagrams, XY-plot views, and more\nL47@P1: • Data logging, voltmeter display, and in-app scripting\nL48@P1: Software Support\nL49@P1: • WaveForms, Digilent's free software application for Windows, Mac, and Linux\nL50@P1: • WaveForms SDK for custom applications and scripting through C/C++, Python, C#, Visual Basic\nL51@P1-2: • Digilent WaveForms VIs for NI LabVIEWAnalog Discovery 3 Datasheet – Rev 1, May 2025\nL52@P2: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL53@P2: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 3 of 26\nL54@P2: Connectors\nL55@P2: 2x15 MTE Connector for External Signals\nL56@P2: USB-C and Auxiliary Power Supply\nL57@P2: *The Analog Discovery 3 can be powered over USB and does not require an auxiliary power supply for most\nL58@P2-3: functions.Analog Discovery 3 Datasheet – Rev 1, May 2025\nL59@P3: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL60@P3: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 4 of 26\nL61@P3: 2 WaveForms Software\nL62@P3: Digilent's free WaveForms software offers a unified device experience across all our Test and Measurement\nL63@P3: devices, enabling use of all hardware features and instruments. It features a friendly user interface that has the\nL64@P3: feel of traditional benchtop devices. WaveForms makes it easy to acquire, visualize, store, analyze, produce, and\nL65@P3: reuse both analog and digital signals simultaneously.\nL66@P3: For even more customization potential, the free WaveForms Software Development Kit (SDK) can be used to\nL67@P3: create custom applications and scripts to control the T&M device in Python, C, and additional languages.\nL68@P3: WaveForms is Windows, Mac, and Linux compatible.\nL69@P3: 2.1 Oscilloscope\nL70@P3: The Oscilloscope instrument captures analog input data\nL71@P3: via the analog input scope channels. When this instrument\nL72@P3: is used, the Analog Discovery 3's analog input channels act\nL73@P3: as a two channel, 14-bit, 125 MS/s oscilloscope. Multiple\nL74@P3: sample and triggering modes are supported.\nL75@P3: 2.2 Waveform Generator\nL76@P3: The Waveform Generator instrument can output analog\nL77@P3: voltage waveforms. The instrument supports everything\nL78@P3: from simple waveforms like Sine and Triangle waves, up\nL79@P3: to more complicated functions like AM and FM\nL80@P3: modulation. Custom sets of samples can be defined by the\nL81@P3: user in applications like Excel and imported to\nL82@P3: WaveForms.\nL83@P3: 2.3 Power Supplies\nL84@P3: The Analog Discovery Studio Max has two variable power\nL85@P3: supply rails that can be used to power your circuits. These\nL86@P3: rails can be set to voltage levels between 0.5 V to 5 V and\nL87@P3-4: -0.5 V to -5 V.Analog Discovery 3 Datasheet – Rev 1, May 2025\nL88@P4: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL89@P4: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 5 of 26\nL90@P4: 2.4 Voltmeter\nL91@P4: The Analog Discovery 3's two oscilloscope channels can be\nL92@P4: used with WaveForms' Voltmeter instrument to act as a\nL93@P4: simple voltmeter. DC voltages, AC RMS voltages, and True\nL94@P4: RMS voltages can be viewed for each channel.\nL95@P4: 2.5 Data Logger\nL96@P4: The Data Logger instrument can capture large buffers of\nL97@P4: analog input data on the Scope pins. The Data Logger can\nL98@P4: capture buffers of data at update rates of up to 1000\nL99@P4: samples per second. The maximum duration of a log is\nL100@P4: dependent on the update rate, but at the extreme, can\nL101@P4: run for over a thousand hours.\nL102@P4: 2.6 Logic Analyzer\nL103@P4: With the Logic Analyzer, the 16 digital input/output\nL104@P4: channels are configured to capture high/low logic states.\nL105@P4: These 3.3 V channels are tolerant to voltages of up to 5 V.\nL106@P4: Individual input/output channels can be grouped as buses\nL107@P4: and protocols. Protocol groups can be used to view the\nL108@P4: decoded contents of packets of many common\nL109@P4: communications protocols, including SPI, I2C, UART, CAN,\nL110@P4: and I2S.\nL111@P4: Signal states, decoded bus values, and decoded protocols\nL112@P4: can be used to trigger a Logic Analyzer capture. Protocol\nL113@P4: triggers include protocol-specific events, like start-of\u0002transmission, end-of-transmission, or packet contents\nL114@P4-5: matching a user-specified value.Analog Discovery 3 Datasheet – Rev 1, May 2025\nL115@P5: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL116@P5: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 6 of 26\nL117@P5: 2.7 Pattern Generator\nL118@P5: The Pattern Generator can generate logic signal\nL119@P5: sequences on the digital input/output pins. The pins can\nL120@P5: be configured to be push/pull, open drain, open source, or\nL121@P5: three-state logic.\nL122@P5: Various patterns can be generated, including clocks,\nL123@P5: random signals, multiple counter types, and fully custom\nL124@P5: digital data. ROM logic can map digital input pins to digital\nL125@P5: outputs, using user-defined truth tables.\nL126@P5: 2.8 Static I/O\nL127@P5: The Static I/O instrument can emulate a variety of user\nL128@P5: input/output devices on the digital input/output pins.\nL129@P5: Virtual LEDs, buttons, switches, sliders, and displays can\nL130@P5: be assigned to specific digital I/O pins and interacted with\nL131@P5: within the WaveForms user interface.\nL132@P5: 2.9 Spectrum Analyzer\nL133@P5: The Spectrum Analyzer instrument is used to view the\nL134@P5: power of frequency-domain components of analog signals\nL135@P5: captured on the analog input channels. Cursors and\nL136@P5: automatic measurements include noise floor, SFDR, SNR,\nL137@P5-6: THD and more.Analog Discovery 3 Datasheet – Rev 1, May 2025\nL138@P6: Copyright Digilent, Inc. All rights reserved.\nL139@P6: Other product and company names mentioned may be trademarks of their respective owners. Page 7 of 26\nL140@P6: 2.10 Network Analyzer\nL141@P6: The Network Analyzer instrument can be used to view the\nL142@P6: amplitude and phase response of a circuit under test.\nL143@P6: Bode, Nichols, and Nyquist plots can also be viewed with\nL144@P6: this instrument. The Network Analyzer instrument uses\nL145@P6: the analog output and analog input channels of the Analog\nL146@P6: Discovery Studio Max to probe a test circuit, by generating\nL147@P6: a frequency sweep and measuring the circuit’s response.\nL148@P6: The Network Analyzer can be configured to use an\nL149@P6: external signal to provide input to the circuit under test,\nL150@P6: rather than using the analog output channels.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnalog Discovery 3: 125 MS/s USB Oscilloscope, Waveform Generator, Logic Analyzer, and Variable Power Supply - Digilent (https://digilent.com/shop/analog-discovery-3/)\nciteturn11view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://digilent.com/shop/analog-discovery-3/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 460\nL0: 【0†Image: null†www.facebook.com】 【1†Image†www.facebook.com】 【2†Toggle menu】\nL1: \nL2:   * 【3†Shop Products】\nL3: \nL4: 【4†Find Reference Materials】\nL5:   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   * 【2†Currency: USD】\nL6:     * 【5†US Dollar】\nL7:     * 【6†Australian dollar】\nL8:     * 【7†British pound】\nL9:     * 【8†Canadian dollar】\nL10:     * 【9†Chinese yuan】\nL11:     * 【10†Euro】\nL12:     * 【11†Indian rupee】\nL13:     * 【12†Japanese yen】\nL14:     * 【13†Malaysian ringgit】\nL15:     * 【14†New Zealand dollar】\nL16:     * 【15†Singapore dollar】\nL17:     * 【16†South Korean won】\nL18:     * 【17†Czech Republic koruna】\nL19:     * 【18†Danish krone】\nL20:     * 【19†Hungarian forint】\nL21:     * 【20†Mexican peso】\nL22:     * 【21†Norwegian krone】\nL23:     * 【22†Polish zloty】\nL24:     * 【23†Romanian leu】\nL25:     * 【24†Swedish krona】\nL26:     * 【25†Swiss franc】\nL27:     * 【26†New Taiwan dollar】\nL28:       * Search Keyword:\nL29: \nL30: [Input: Search Products]\nL31: \nL32:     * 【27†Contact Us】\nL33: \nL34: Search [Input: Search Products]\nL35: \nL36:   * 【2†Currency: USD】\nL37:     * 【5†US Dollar】\nL38:     * 【6†Australian dollar】\nL39:     * 【7†British pound】\nL40:     * 【8†Canadian dollar】\nL41:     * 【9†Chinese yuan】\nL42:     * 【10†Euro】\nL43:     * 【11†Indian rupee】\nL44:     * 【12†Japanese yen】\nL45:     * 【13†Malaysian ringgit】\nL46:     * 【14†New Zealand dollar】\nL47:     * 【15†Singapore dollar】\nL48:     * 【16†South Korean won】\nL49:     * 【17†Czech Republic koruna】\nL50:     * 【18†Danish krone】\nL51:     * 【19†Hungarian forint】\nL52:     * 【20†Mexican peso】\nL53:     * 【21†Norwegian krone】\nL54:     * 【22†Polish zloty】\nL55:     * 【23†Romanian leu】\nL56:     * 【24†Swedish krona】\nL57:     * 【25†Swiss franc】\nL58:     * 【26†New Taiwan dollar】\nL59:   * 【28†Sign in†cloud.digilent.com】 or 【29†Register†cloud.digilent.com】\nL60: \nL61: Search Keyword:\nL62: \nL63: [Input: Search Products]\nL64: \nL65:   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   * # Analog Discovery 3: 125 MS/s USB Oscilloscope, Waveform Generator, Logic Analyzer, and Variable Power Supply\nL66: \nL67: MSRP:\nL68: \nL69: Was:\nL70: \nL71: Now: (Inc. Tax) (Including Tax)\nL72: \nL73: MSRP:\nL74: \nL75: Was:\nL76: \nL77: Now: $379.00\nL78: \nL79: (You save )\nL80: \nL81: 【30†(10 reviews)】 【30†Write a Review】\nL82: #### Write a Review\nL83: \nL84: Close ×\nL85: \nL86: 【31†Image: Product image of the Analog Discovery 3, displayed at an angle.†cdn11.bigcommerce.com】\nL87: ##### Analog Discovery 3: 125 MS/s USB Oscilloscope, Waveform Generator, Logic Analyzer, and Variable Power Supply\nL88: \nL89: Rating Required [Select]\nL90: \nL91: Name Required [Input]\nL92: \nL93: Review Subject Required [Input]\nL94: \nL95: Comments Required\nL96: \nL97: [Input]\nL98: \nL99: [Input] [Input]\nL100: \nL101: SKU:\nL102:     410-415\nL103: UPC:\nL104: \nL105: \nL106: [Input] [Input]\nL107: \nL108: Analog Discovery 3 options: (Required) [Input] Analog Discovery 3 [Input] Analog Discovery 3 - ACADEMIC ONLY (Academic verification with distributor is required)\nL109: \nL110: Purchase from one of our distributors listed below:\nL111:   * 【32†Description】\nL112:   * 【33†10 Reviews】\nL113: \nL114: Looking to buy the Analog Discovery 3 along with accessories? Check out our 【34†Pro Bundle】 or 【35†Student Bundle】!\nL115: ## Discover More with the New Analog Discovery 3\nL116: \nL117: Digilent’s latest multi-function test and measurement device, also known as the AD3, is a digital oscilloscope, logic analyzer, waveform generator, pattern generator, and much more. Using the flexible WaveForms software (supported by Windows, Mac, and Linux), the Analog Discovery 3 can be used in the lab, in the field, or even at home - you're no longer tied down to a traditional benchtop and stacks of expensive test instruments.\nL118: ## Portability\nL119: \nL120: Like its predecessor, the Analog Discovery 3 is a portable USB-powered test and measurement device, though it now features an upgraded USB-C^{®} connection. It’s lightweight and small enough to fit in your pocket or backpack, so it becomes an exceptional companion for any engineer.\nL121: ## Expanded WaveForms Features\nL122: The Analog Discovery 3 boasts a larger-than-ever buffer size, allowing more data to be sent through the waveform generator and received through the mixed signal (analog and digital) oscilloscope. It also has an increased sampling rate of up to 125 MS/s on all channels and a more potent power supply (up to 800 mA). The larger buffers allow you to capture longer records at high resolution and enable more complex AWG signals.\nL123: Modulated AWG signals also take advantage of larger buffers, and more complex modulated signals have been added including phase modulation and summation.\nL124: This iteration of our most popular Analog Discovery digital oscilloscope also allows engineers access to new powerful features in WaveForms like the FIR (finite impulse response) Filter. Through the use of optional FIR filters on Oscilloscope input channels, users can reduce the amount of high frequency noise that could affect the quality of their measurements. Digital loopback of captured signals to the AWG, both filtered and unfiltered, is now supported.\nL125: AWG output signals can now also be recaptured through digital loopback and displayed alongside signals returned from a circuit under test.\nL126: The AD3 is built on the next generation AMD FPGA, opening up even more features and future enhancements over time with software updates.\nL127: ## Accessories\nL128: \nL129: The Analog Discovery 3 has access to all of the adapters and accessories from its previous version, as well as the new 【36†Audio Adapter+】. These accessories, like the 【37†Transistor Tester】, 【38†Impedance Analyzer】, and 【39†Breadboard Adapter】, are available to buy from our online store.\nL130: \nL131: If you’re looking to buy an oscilloscope, the AD3 is a digital oscilloscope option that is a complete solution, meaning that you won’t have to shop for more benchtop equipment.\nL132: The Analog Discovery 3 is proudly manufactured using Texas Instruments components.\nL133: \nL134: Oscilloscope:\nL135: \nL136:   * Two differential channels with 14-bit resolution at up to 125 MS/s per channel with a +/-25 V input range, 30+ MHz bandwidth with BNC Adapter\nL137:   * User-configurable hardware input filters\nL138:   * FFT, Spectrogram, Lock-In Amplifier, Additional Software Input Filters, Eye Diagram, XY Plot views, and more, in the WaveForms application\nL139: \nL140: Arbitrary Waveform Generator:\nL141:   * Two channels with 14-bit resolution at up to 125 MS/s per channel with a +/-5 V output range, 12 MHz bandwidth with BNC Adapter\nL142:   * Standard waveforms, amplitude and frequency modulated signals, direct playback from analog inputs, custom waveforms, and more\nL143: \nL144: Logic Analyzer and Pattern Generator:\nL145:   * 16 digital I/O channels at up to 125 MS/s per channel\nL146:   * Individually-configurable 3.3 V digital inputs and outputs, 5 V tolerant inputs\nL147:   * SPI, I2C, UART, CAN, JTAG, ROM logic, custom protocols, and more\nL148: \nL149: Programmable Power Supplies:\nL150: \nL151:   * 0.5 V to 5 V and -0.5 V to -5 V variable power supplies\nL152:   * Up to 800 mA per channel when used with an 【40†auxiliary power source】\nL153: \nL154: Additional Features:\nL155:   * Additional software instruments including:\nL156:     * Spectrum Analyzer, Network Analyzer, and Impedance Analyzer\nL157:     * Protocol Analyzer, virtual digital I/O such as buttons, switches, LEDs\nL158:     * Data logging, Voltmeter, in-app scripting\nL159:   * SDK for hardware control in C, C++, Python, and other languages\nL160:   * Support for LabVIEW and MATLAB\nL161:   * And much more\nL162: \nL163: Product Compliance:\nL164: \nL165:   * HTC: 8471809000\nL166:   * ECCN: 3A992.a\nL167:   * 【41†Declaration of Conformity†files.digilent.com】\nL168: *Note: To achieve maximum bandwidth, use of the 【42†BNC Adapter】 for Analog Discovery is required.\nL169: \nL170: What's Included:\nL171: \nL172:   * Analog Discovery 3\nL173:   * One plastic project box\nL174:   * One USB-C^{®} to USB-C programming cable\nL175:   * One 【43†2x15 flywire signal cable assembly】\nL176:   * 【44†5-pack of 6-pin male headers】\nL177:   * 【45†30-pin flywire labels】\nL178: \nL179: Note: the Academic Only option (SKU: 410-415-1) requires academic verification with the chosen distributor. Kit contents are the same.\nL180: Recommended Accessories (Not Included with Analog Discovery 3):\nL181: \nL182:   * 【46†Soft Case】 for the Analog Discovery 3\nL183:   * 【40†5 V, 4 A】 Switching Power Supply\nL184:   * 【36†Audio Adapter+】\nL185:   * 【42†BNC Adapter】 along with 【47†P2150 BNC Oscilloscope Probe】\nL186:   * 【37†Transistor Tester】\nL187:   * 【38†Impedance Analyzer】\nL188:   * 【39†Breadboard Adapter】\nL189: ## What is WaveForms?\nL190: WaveForms is the free software application for the Analog Discovery 3 and enables use of the available analog and digital instruments. The software has been refined by customer feedback for over 10 years and features a computer and laptop friendly user interface that has the feel of traditional benchtop software. The Analog Discovery 3 communicates with WaveForms via a USB connection to your computer, allowing users to capture, record, analyze, and generate mixed signal and mixed domain waveforms.\nL191: WaveForms can be tested without hardware using its demo mode feature. In addition to the use of instruments in the application, the WaveForms application has a script editor tool, which allows custom scripting of the instrument in JavaScript. WaveForms is designed to be run on a laptop or desktop computer and is Mac, Windows, and Linux compatible.\n"